<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Kiprono maximised <m>P = {9}x + {7}y</m> on the region R given by <m>4x + 7y \le 46</m>, <m>9x + 2y \le 65</m>, <m>x \ge 1</m>, <m>y \ge 1</m>, with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> whole numbers. His working:</p><p>Step 1: The vertices of R are <m>(1, 1)</m>, <m>(7, 1)</m>, <m>(6.6, 2.8)</m>, <m>(1, 6)</m>.</p><p>Step 2: <m>P</m> at the vertices is <m>16</m>, <m>70</m>, <m>79</m>, <m>51</m>.</p><p>Step 3: The best vertex is <m>(6.6, 2.8)</m>; to the nearest whole numbers this is <m>(7, 3)</m>.</p><p>Step 4: The greatest value is <m>P = 84</m>, at <m>(7, 3)</m>.</p><p>(a) Which statement describes his mistake?</p><div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>His working is correct.</li>
<li>He rounded the vertex to the nearest whole numbers, but that point is outside R.</li>
<li>He rounded both numbers down, but another whole-number point gives more.</li>
<li>He chose the vertex with the smallest value of P.</li>
<li>His answer is the value at a vertex that is not a whole-number point.</li>
</ol>  </div><p>(b) What is the greatest value of <m>P</m> with whole numbers? <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/>  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) He rounded the vertex to the nearest whole numbers, but that point is outside R.</p><p>(b) A vertex that is not a whole-number point is not an answer: test the whole-number points of R around the best vertex, and check every constraint. The best is <m>({6}, {3})</m>, with <m>P = {75}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>75</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>